      "title": "2. The Profit and Loss Account: Trading Account, Gross Profit and COGS",
      "content": "The **profit and loss account** (or **income statement**) is a financial statement of a firm's **trading activities over a period of time**, usually one year. Its main purpose is to show the **value of profit or loss** during a trading period. Even **non-profit organizations** and **public sector** organizations need a **financial surplus** to survive.\n\n* **Profit**, in its simplest form, is the positive difference between a firm's revenues and its costs.\n* **Revenues** are inflows of money from ordinary trading activities: cash sales, credit sales, charges/fees and royalties.\n* **Costs** are outflows of money arising from operations: wages, salaries, rents and the purchase of stocks (inventories).\n\nThe P&L account has **three sections**: (i) the **trading account**, (ii) the **profit and loss account** and (iii) the **appropriation account**.\n\n### (i) The Trading Account\nThe trading account shows the difference between **sales revenue** (the value of products sold to customers) and the costs of producing or purchasing those products. It shows **gross profit**:\n\n> **Formula:** `Gross profit = Sales revenue − Cost of goods sold`\n\n**Cost of goods sold (COGS)** (or **cost of sales**, COS, for services) is the accountant's term for the **direct costs of the goods actually sold**, such as raw material costs:\n\n> **Formula:** `COGS = Opening stock + Purchases − Closing stock`\n\n**Closing stock** is the value of stock (inventory) remaining at the end of the trading period; it is deducted when calculating COGS because it has not been sold.",
