      "id": "sec-3.4-pl-full",
      "title": "3. Net Profit, Interest & Tax, and the Appropriation Account",
      "content": "### (ii) The Profit and Loss Section\n**Net profit** is the financial surplus from sales revenue **after all costs and expenses** are accounted for, i.e. the actual profit from normal trading activities.\n\n> **Formula:** `Net profit = Gross profit − Expenses`\n\n**Expenses** are the **indirect or fixed costs** of production, such as administration charges, management salaries, insurance premiums (for buildings, vehicles and stock), rent of land and property, and stationery costs. **Negative numbers are shown in brackets** in the P&L account.\n\n**Florists-R-Us** sold stock costing **$230,000** for **$460,000** in the fiscal year ended 31st December 2022. Rents were **$90,000**, utility bills **$60,000** and other overheads **$15,000**.\n\n| Profit statement for Florists-R-Us, year ended 31st Dec 2022 | $ | $ |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Sales revenue | | 460,000 |\n| Cost of goods sold (COGS) | | (230,000) |\n| **Gross profit** | | **230,000** |\n| Less expenses: Rents | (90,000) | |\n| Utility bills | (60,000) | |\n| Other overheads | (15,000) | (165,000) |\n| **Net profit** | | **65,000** |\n\n### Interest and Tax\nInterest and tax, despite being expenses, are shown as **separate items** because both **interest and tax rates change over time and are beyond the control of the business**. Recording **profit before interest and tax** allows firms to **compare like with like** and make historical comparisons.\n* **Interest** is the charge a business pays to its financiers, such as commercial banks.\n* **Corporate tax** is the levy payable on a company's profits.\n\n### (iii) The Appropriation Account\nThe appropriation account shows how **net profit after interest and tax** is distributed:\n* **Dividends**: the amount distributed to the **owners (shareholders)**. The proportion is decided by the **Board of Directors** and approved at the **Annual General Meeting**. Dividends are usually paid **bi-annually**: an **interim dividend** midway through the year and a **final dividend** at the end of the fiscal year.\n* **Retained profit**: how much of the profit after interest and tax is **kept by the business** as an **internal source of finance** (e.g. reinvestment or expansion). For non-profit organizations the term **retained surplus** is used. This figure is transferred to the **'Equity'** section of the balance sheet as **'retained earnings'**.\n\n### Full P&L Account: Florists-R-Us (pp. 254-255)\nFlorists-R-Us has **$10,000** interest, tax at **10%**, pays **30%** of profit after interest and tax as dividends and retains the rest.\n\n| Profit & loss account for Florists-R-Us, year ended 31 December | $ | Section |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Sales revenue | 460,000 | Trading account |\n| Cost of sales | (230,000) | Trading account |\n| **Gross profit** | **230,000** | Trading account |\n| Expenses | (165,000) | P&L account |\n| **Profit before interest and tax** | **65,000** | P&L account |\n| Interest | (10,000) | P&L account |\n| **Profit before tax** | **55,000** | P&L account |\n| Tax (10%) | (5,500) | P&L account |\n| **Profit after interest and tax** | **49,500** | P&L account |\n| Dividends (30%) | (14,850) | Appropriation account |\n| **Retained profit (70%)** | **34,650** | Appropriation account |\n\n![Worked example - Florists-R-Us statement of profit or loss with $10,000 interest and 10% tax](/finance/unit-3-4-income-statement-worked-example.png)\n*Source: Hoang (2022), worked example (Florists-R-Us), p.254.*\n\n### IB Format: Table 17.1 (profit-making) vs Table 17.2 (non-profit)\n\n| Table 17.1: Profit-making organization | $m | Table 17.2: Non-profit organization | $m |\n|---|---:|---|---:|\n| Sales revenue | 700 | Sales revenue | 700 |\n| Cost of sales | (350) | Cost of sales | (350) |\n| **Gross profit** | **350** | **Gross surplus** | **350** |\n| Less expenses | (200) | Less expenses | (200) |\n| **Profit before interest and tax** | **150** | **Surplus before interest and tax** | **150** |\n| Less interest | (10) | Less interest | (10) |\n| **Profit before tax** | **140** | **Surplus before tax** | **140** |\n| Less tax | (25) | Less tax | (25) |\n| **Profit for period** | **115** | **Surplus for period** | **115** |\n| Dividends | (35) | (no dividends: no shareholders) | |\n| **Retained profit** | **80** | **Retained surplus** | **115** |\n\n![Table 17.1 - IB format of the statement of profit or loss for a profit-making organization](/finance/unit-3-4-income-statement-format.png)\n*Source: Hoang (2022), Table 17.1, p.254.*\n\n![Table 17.2 - Statement of profit or loss format for a non-profit organization (surplus)](/finance/unit-3-4-income-statement-npo-format.png)\n*Source: Hoang (2022), Table 17.2, p.254.*\n\n### Ways to Reduce Expenses and Raise Net Profit\n* **Rent**: negotiate charges or move to cheaper premises (though relocation may be unfeasible due to **industrial inertia**).\n* **Fuel consumption**: reduce heating, air conditioning and lighting, which cuts electricity bills and helps protect the natural environment.\n* **Administration costs**: review clerical work, **combine jobs**, employ fewer people, or **outsource** some jobs.",
