      "content": "The P&L account is important because it shows the profit (or loss) **after all costs and expenses** are accounted for. Gross profit might look appealing, but if **expenses exceed gross profit**, the business makes an overall **loss**. A business cannot survive for long without making actual profit.\n\n### Limitations of the P&L Account\n\n| Limitation | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| **Historical** | Shows past performance; there is **no guarantee** future performance is linked to past success |\n| **No international standard format** | Difficult to compare the profit or loss of firms in different countries, in the same or different industries |\n| **Window dressing** can occur | Firms may include the **sale of some fixed assets** or **non-operating income** to boost profits and impress shareholders |\n\n**Window dressing** refers to the **legal** act of **creative accounting** by manipulating financial data (final accounts) to make the results appear more appealing, even though this **disguises the underlying financial position** of the company (Figure 17.2).",
