      "title": "8. Intangible Assets",
      "content": "**Intangible assets** are **non-physical** noncurrent (fixed) assets that are of monetary value and can **earn revenue**, such as brand names, goodwill, trademarks, copyrights and patents. They are legally protected by **intellectual property rights (IPR)**. They can be a large proportion of a firm's asset value, but it is usually **difficult to place an objective and accurate price** on them.\n\n| Intangible asset | Meaning | Textbook example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **(i) Branding** | Brand recognition and brand value drive sales; an **indefinite asset** because recognition and loyalty stay for as long as the company exists | Apple brand worth **$264bn** in 2021 (Statista), the most valuable that year; Coca-Cola |\n| **(ii) Patents** | Legal protection for **inventors**, preventing copying for a **fixed number of years**; gives **exclusive rights** to commercial production; others must apply and **pay a fee**. An **incentive to innovate** (R&D) | Compact discs patented by **Philips** |\n| **(iii) Copyrights** | Legal protection for the **original artistic work** of a creator (author, photographer, painter, musician); reproducing or modifying needs **permission**, usually for a fee | Newspapers, sound recordings, software, movies; the textbook itself (author and IBID Press) |\n| **(iv) Goodwill** | Exists when the **value of a firm exceeds its book value** (net assets); includes reputation, corporate image, business connections, and customer and employee loyalty | Marshall Field: \"Goodwill is the one and only asset that competition cannot undersell or destroy\"; Boots (2022) valued between **£5bn and £12bn** |\n| **(v) Registered trademarks** | **Distinctive signs** (names, symbols, phrases, images) that uniquely identify a brand, product or business; legally protected and **can be sold** | Nike 'swoosh', Audi's four rings; Volkswagen bought **Bentley** for **£430m ($615m)** in 1998 |\n\n### Why Intangible Assets Are Often Not Recorded\n* Their value is **very difficult to measure objectively** (What are the Google or Coca-Cola brands really worth? How do we value employee goodwill in a school or hospital?).\n* **Goodwill is only truly realised** when an acquisition or takeover happens and the buyer pays a **premium**: the difference between the value of the business and the purchase price.\n* Adding intangibles can be a form of **window dressing** to artificially inflate the value of a business: legal, but it can **distort** financial health. **Vodafone** turned a **€11bn ($15bn) loss** into a **€10bn profit** in 2006 by including goodwill.\n* TOK: in 2014 **Forbes** valued Google's brand at about **$47bn**, while **Interbrand** valued it at over **$93bn**.\n\n### Intangibles and Strategy\nDeveloping **IPRs** (patents, copyrights) can act as a **barrier to entry** (Coca-Cola, Toyota, The Walt Disney Company) and protect market share sustainably. **Aristotle Onassis** said the secret to success is to have or know something no one else does, so **creativity** and **new product development** matter. Many firms now see IPRs as an **offensive**, not just defensive, strategy.",
